Forward-declare all plc-emitted struct typedefs in dependencies.plc.h.

plc emits each POU's C header as `typedef struct { ... } NAME;` (anonymous
struct with typedef alias) and orders emissions by source order in the .st
file rather than by dependency. When one struct references a later-defined
one via a pointer -- e.g. `GEN_CRC16_type` in mGENx.h has field
`GEN_CRC16_TABLE_type* CRC16_TABLE;` while `GEN_CRC16_TABLE_type` is defined
further down -- the C compile fails with "unknown type name". Same hazard
applies across modules (mCOPx.h references CAN_CH_INIT_type from mCANx.h,
etc.).

Fix in two coordinated changes, both driven by generate_headers.py:

1. After `plc --generate-headers`, rewrite each emitted `<name>.h` to give
   every anonymous struct typedef a matching struct tag:
       typedef struct { ... } NAME;
     becomes
       typedef struct NAME { ... } NAME;

2. Collect every such NAME across the headers dir and render them into
   dependencies.plc.h as forward typedef declarations:
       typedef struct NAME NAME;
   Each generated header unconditionally #includes <dependencies.plc.h>
   as its first include, so any pointer to NAME is valid before NAME's
   struct body is seen.

C11 allows redundant typedef declarations that refer to the same type
(section 6.7 p3), so the forward `typedef struct NAME NAME;` and the later
`typedef struct NAME { ... } NAME;` in the same translation unit are both
well-formed.

The plc-emitted header itself has no unions or enums; only struct typedefs,
one per POU, without nesting. A simple non-greedy `\{[^{}]*\}` regex is
sufficient to locate each block.
--- a/st/private/dependencies.plc.h.jinja
+++ b/st/private/dependencies.plc.h.jinja
@@ -80,6 +80,17 @@
     double lreal_;
 #endif
 } AnyNumValue;
+{% if forward_declarations %}
+// Forward typedefs for every POU struct in this library, so any header's
+// pointer to a struct defined further down (in the same file or in another
+// module's file) resolves regardless of emission order. Paired with the tag
+// injection in generate_headers.py, which rewrites the eventual struct body
+// to `typedef struct NAME { ... } NAME;` -- a redundant typedef of NAME to
+// the same `struct NAME` type, allowed by C11 6.7.p3.
+{% for name in forward_declarations -%}
+typedef struct {{ name }} {{ name }};
+{% endfor -%}
+{% endif -%}
 {% if auto_includes %}
 // Other (declaration-only) headers generated alongside this one.
 {% for name in auto_includes -%}
